186078960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078608 #edb/ref 1039 "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation: Word Problem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078608 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078608 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bf9fa7f8-708a-5006-9b9b-ea2c0334cb2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078662 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078662 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078662 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078662 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078662 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078662 #edb/ref 1045 "Estimating Derivatives Using a Central Difference Quotient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078662 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078662 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078662 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186078662 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "44cc6885-bb82-5b20-bcaf-40d9c657e036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079578 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079578 #edb/ref 1061 695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079578 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a60aa634-136d-5259-b7e2-330e5586dcf6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079578 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079579 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079579 #edb/ref 1061 699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079579 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dc49f5fa-24d5-522d-94f8-c7cb90c5dfd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079579 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079580 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079580 #edb/ref 1061 701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079580 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa5bcebe-0da6-5538-9ccd-5d348315d13a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079580 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079581 #edb/ref 1061 703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079581 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a5d43107-12f5-564b-8b7e-258d9b82d9da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079581 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079668 #edb/ref 1064 "We've learned two methods for approximating the value of the derivative at a point by computing the rate of change between two points a distance of $h$ apart, where $h$ is small.\n\n- The forward difference approximation computes the rate of change between the point $x$ and the point $x+h,$ which is a step of $h$ units forward.\n\n$$\nf'(x)  \\approx \\dfrac{f(x+h)- f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n- The backward difference approximation computes the rate of change between the point $x$ and the point $x-h,$ which is a step of $h$ units backward.\n\n$$\nf'(x)  \\approx \\dfrac{f(x)- f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, we will introduce the **central difference approximation**, also known as the **symmetric difference approximation**. In this approximation, we compute the rate of change between the points $x-h$ and $x+h,$ a distance of $2h$ apart.\n\n$$\nf'(x)  \\approx \\dfrac{f(x+h)- f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n![](images/48/48414a74d6f06fdec0f9b07c2a14f42c3a528b578b3a5f7a61cb4eff93016ac2.png)\n\nUsually, the central difference approximation is a better approximation for $f '(x)$ than the forward difference approximation and the backward difference approximation. This is because for the central difference approximation, $x$ is the midpoint between the two points $x-h$ and $x+h$ that are used in the approximation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079668 #edb/ref 1065 372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079668 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6ac17418-b32e-5d12-97ef-d94adc56f308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079668 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281898 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281898 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281898 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "167457a9-b6b4-5440-b129-2f0689b6e02a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282109 #edb/ref 1080 "5.2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282109 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282109 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2f6ae62d-82cc-507a-a390-da0ec1363bb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282125 #edb/ref 1080 "6.8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282125 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282125 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "30d44fd4-c8bf-5a7d-8db2-4407697e9981" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282400 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282400 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282400 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "534f1b55-dd7e-54a5-b639-76254d4ef738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283281 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283281 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283281 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b830ca2d-56fe-5d6d-b7b4-790849ecdebc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186281898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284065 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284065 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284065 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186282400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284065 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4f3db73c-bd8b-5985-8c46-305bfc25d447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284662 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284662 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170649" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284662 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284662 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(2.9)=5.91$ and $f(3.1)=7.11,$ find an approximation for ${f}^{′}(3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284662 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a448f94c-9203-5aa7-907f-0bb1a9165bbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284662 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=3$ with the step size $h=0.1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3+0.1)-f(3-0.1)}{2(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3.1)-f(2.9)}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7.11-5.91}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.2}{0.2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296148 #edb/ref 1080 "-0.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296148 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296148 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a7e2085a-e531-5baf-8fe5-1d1d7a6ad9e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296904 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186296148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296904 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296904 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296904 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186296148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296904 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8369a49e-b4e6-517f-b327-76c2f9620e9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297414 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297414 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297414 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297414 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d2/d27b0d70171683240fd44c8bbbcc8cb46ce0f0f216533a8061d31a991cce4e14.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=1$ and $x=3.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297414 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4894e4a4-bc69-5cbe-8eef-c136c4c17945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297414 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=2$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{f(2+1)-f(2-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3)-f(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316180 #edb/ref 1080 "-0.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316180 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316180 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bba96510-3aad-52e6-933c-3020fe0f7265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316803 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316803 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316803 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316803 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316803 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6a7d58ba-9fa1-5287-b687-96086e2fe893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317459 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317459 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317459 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317459 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/56/5663e7c59a9f42b31ebadd9abb54e36c604526c00334f840014a674c5b6dcdb8.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=3.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=3$ and $x=9.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(6)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317459 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe82b330-59ab-5f93-9994-eddddff07551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317459 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=6$ with step size $h=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{f(6+3)-f(6-3)}{2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(9)-f(3)}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)}{6} \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330126 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330126 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330126 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15a00187-cbc3-5317-b9e6-87f2abfe2bd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330259 #edb/ref 1080 "1.1\\,\\text{lb}/\\text{week}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330259 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330259 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "24265d78-819c-5988-b582-ea8cc29f061c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330359 #edb/ref 1080 "1\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330359 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330359 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e9de2d0-f9be-5cb4-96dd-ac8265f872ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330586 #edb/ref 1080 "1.05\\,\\text{lb}/\\text{week}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330586 #edb/ref