332286 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332286 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332286 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332286 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332286 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186330259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332286 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "31d4a9e5-c745-5774-b2c2-c53d83fc4b40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332288 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332288 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332288 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332288 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332288 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186332020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332288 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332288 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332288 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186330359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332288 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3286ba04-ae3e-5cab-8f4c-55cb8cefd221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332878 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332878 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51402" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332878 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332878 #edb/ref 1071 "Sam wanted to estimate the rate of weight growth of his new puppy. He took note of its weight every week, for $7$ weeks. The results are shown in the table below, where $W(t)$ is the puppy's weight (measured in pounds) after $t$ weeks from birth.\n\n| **$t$** | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ | $13$ | $14$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $W(t)$ | $9$ | $10.1$ | $11.3$ | $12.4$ | $13.5$ | $14.6$ | $15.8$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate the puppy's rate of weight growth $12$ weeks after birth using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332878 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c6e93bc-4fb5-52a4-975a-8dfbc53642f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332878 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{W(t+h)-W(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=12$ and step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(0.2) & ≈\\frac{W(12+1)-W(12-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{W(13)-W(11)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14.6-12.4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.2}{2} \\\\  & =1.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the rate of weight growth at the instant of time $t=12$ is $1.1\\,\\text{lb}/\\text{week}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332879 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332879 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332879 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332879 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1e/1e11260301be8c0bb19a9fd47ffbbf71cc5fa4d94dbeae57af934b76fb17f0e1.png)\n\nA particle has a speed of $v(t)$ meters per second after $t$ seconds. The graph of $v(t)$ is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of speed when $t=3$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332879 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14f1836e-031c-5d94-96ad-23aaab157b74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332879 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{v}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{v(t+h)-g(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2$ and step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{v(3+1)-v(3-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{v(4)-v(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of speed at the instant of time $t=2$ is approximately $1\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429550 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429550 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 640->993. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429550 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186049236 #edb/ref 17592186045436]}
